// Naming a type also names what its fields are made of.
//
// `Arena` carries an allocator, so an emission that declares the arena and not
// the allocator is not merely short a declaration - it is C that does not
// compile, because a struct field needs a complete type. The rule that puts a
// named-but-never-built type in the output therefore has to run to a fixed
// point over field types rather than once per name, and this file is the
// cheapest shape that tells the difference: `Arena` is named in a signature,
// nothing here builds one, and the allocator is reached only through the
// arena's field.
//
// The user enum of the same name is here for the same reason as in the
// allocator fixture next door: it makes the binding observable. Both compilers
// accept the file either way, so parity of the emitted C is the assertion.

enum Arena { A | B }

fn takes(x: *const Arena) { println(1); }

fn main() { println(1); }
